SUMMARY:Exhibition Opening Reception: "Hidden Layers: AI Infrastructures a
 nd Hallucinations"
LOCATION:Worth Ryder Art Gallery\, Anthropology &amp\; Art Building\, Rm 1
 16
DESCRIPTION:The Department of German and the Department of Art Practice ar
 e excited to announce the opening of Hidden Layers: AI Infrastructures and
  Hallucinations\, curated by Antonio Somaini with Hannes Bajohr\, Sonja Th
 iel and Elena Yu\, in dialogue with the France&#8211\;Berkeley research in
 itiative The Latent Spaces of Culture\, and on display at the Worth Ryder 
 Art Gallery.In neural networks\, the "hidden layers" are the intermediate 
 strata of transformation between input and output — the computational st
 ructures where data are transformed\, weighted\, and recombined into incre
 asingly abstract\, distributed representations that no human eye can read.
  The exhibition Hidden Layers:&#160\;AI Infrastructures and Hallucinations
  takes this term as its conceptual frame\, bringing together seventeen int
 ernational artists and researchers working at the intersection of contempo
 rary art and machine learning to make visible what artificial intelligence
  keeps out of sight: its planetary infrastructures and computational laten
 t spaces\, its colonial and ideological underpinnings\, its misrecognition
 s and hallucinations\, and its transformation of our relation to the past 
 through the silent reprocessing of pre-existing cultural forms. The exhibi
 tion is organized in four sections: "In the Latent Spaces of Culture\," "M
 aterial Infrastructures\," "Colonial Imaginaries: From Misrecognition to G
 enerative Slopaganda\," and "Hallucinations and Psychedelia."Featuring wor
 ks by Nouf Aljowaysir (https://www.noufaljowaysir.com/)\; Gašper and Nina
  Beguš & Metahaven (https://latentspacecraft.antikythera.org/)\; Grégory
  Chatonsky (https://chatonsky.net/)\; Kate Crawford & Vladan Joler (https:
 //anatomyof.ai/)\; Asma Kazmi (https://www.asmakazmi.com/) & Jill Miller (
 https://www.jillmiller.net/)\; Andrea Khôra (https://www.andreakhora.com/
 )\; Occitane Lacurie & Barnabé Sauvage (https://www.schirn.de/en/schirnma
 g/3-questions-for-occitane-lacurie-und-barnabe-sauvage/)\; Greg Niemeyer (
 https://www.gregniemeyer.com/)\; and Mario Santamaria (https://mariosantam
 aria.net/) & Alex Saum-Pascual (https://www.alexsaum.com/).Hidden Layers i
 s a collaboration between the Department of German and the Worth Ryder Art
  Gallery at the Department of Art Practice. It is part of the France–Ber
 keley research initiative The Latent Spaces of Culture: a collaboration be
 tween UC Berkeley\, the Université Sorbonne Nouvelle in Paris\, and the F
 rench National Center for Scientific Research (CNRS).Exhibition Dates: Sep
 tember 3 - October 3\, 2026Opening Reception: Thursday\, September 3\, 4-6
 :30pm\, Worth Ryder Art GalleryArtist Talk\, Gregory Chatonsky "INFRALLUCI
 NATION": Thursday\, September 3\, 6:30-8:00pm\, AAPB 285Film Program\, Pus
 h Buttons and AI (https://bampfa.org/event/push-buttons-and-ai): September
  9\, 7 pm\, BAMPFACuratorial Tour\, led by Antonio Somaini: Friday\, Septe
 mber 11\, 4:30pm\, Worth Ryder Art GalleryWorkshop on Latent Space of Cult
 ure: September 9\, 2pm-4pm\; September 10/11\, 9:30 am-4pm on both days\, 
 470 StephensSalon on AI and Art: September 16\, 6pm– 9pm\, AI Futures La
 bTour of AI: October 3 Reservations required. Information forthcomingClosi
 ng day: October 3\, 12-3pm\, Worth Ryder Art GalleryAdditional events\, de
 tails\, and registration links to be announced. Times subject to change\, 
 please also check the campus calendar (https://events.berkeley.edu/art).Th
 is exhibition is supported by the France-Berkeley Fund of the Institute of
  European Studies\, the Department of German and the Department of Art Pra
 ctice\, together with the Berkeley Center for New Media\, the Berkeley Ins
 titute for Data Science (BIDS)\, Department of Rhetoric\; Antikythera\, th
 e Human Technology Futures (HTF)\, the Pamela P. Fong and Family Distingui
 shed Chair in China Studies\, the Division of Arts & Humanities\, the Town
 send Center for the Humanities and the School of Information\, the Technol
 ogy Network of the Berkeley Economy and Society Initiative (BESI)\, and th
 e Berkeley Art\, Law\, and Finance Project\, hosted by the Berkeley Center
  for Law and Business at Berkeley Law.&#160\;In conjunction with the exhib
 ition\, additional programming is developed in collaboration with Berkeley
  Art Museum and Pacific Film Archive (BAMPFA) and the Institute for Contem
 porary Art San Jose.
